    handler hands it over. A future form adds one [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag and calls [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m. Buys a single place where the key
    name, the JSON shape, and the tracking-only rule are written down. Costs one new file and one global. Matches the
    page's existing no-build, global-script style exactly. [3mTrivial-to-moderate.[0m
  - [1mDirect [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m at each call site[0m — no module; every consumer does [38;5;153mgetItem[39m/[38;5;153msetItem[39m itself. Buys zero new
    structure. Costs exactly the thing you asked for: each future form re-implements the key name and none of them
    inherits the "don't trust this" rule, so a typo'd key fails silently and wrong. [3mTrivial, and I'd argue against it.[0m
  - [1mConvert the page to ES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, real [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m, no globals. Buys proper encapsulation
    and a path toward unifying with the CommonJS code in [38;5;153msrc/[39m. Costs a page-wide change to how every script loads for
    the sake of a two-function module, and module scope means the page stops working over [38;5;153mfile://[39m. [3mModerate.[0m

  My recommendation is the first. It's the smallest change that actually creates the shared thing you described, and it
  doesn't impose a loading-model migration on the page to get there. The third is the right destination if this app ever
  grows a build step — and going from the first to the third later is mechanical, mostly adding [38;5;153mexport[39m keywords, so
  choosing it now costs you nothing you can't recover.

  Two things I'd flag regardless of choice:

  1. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub with no network call, so there's no API response to read a real id from yet. The stub will have
     to fabricate one, clearly marked as placeholder, with the real read wired in when the endpoint is real.
  2. [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m will return null before login and after a failed login. Consumers need a defined answer for "no id yet"
     — I'd propose they log the event with the id omitted rather than substituting a guess.
